<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
        Background media playback.

        A browser that stops the audio the moment you switch apps is not a
        browser people can listen to anything with, and on Android keeping audio
        alive in the background is not a passive thing: it needs a foreground
        service with a media type, and that service needs a notification, which
        on API 33+ needs the user's permission.

        Three permissions, and each one earns its place:

          FOREGROUND_SERVICE               the service itself
          FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PLAYBACK  its type, required from API 34.
                                           Declaring the type narrows what the
                                           service is allowed to do, which is
                                           why Android asks for it separately.
          POST_NOTIFICATIONS               the playback notification, from
                                           API 33. Runtime-granted, so the user
                                           is asked and can refuse.

        WAKE_LOCK is deliberately absent. Audio playback holds the CPU awake
        through the audio focus and foreground service, and a browser taking a
        wake lock of its own is how a browser ends up blamed for a flat battery.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PLAYBACK" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />


    <!--
        Guards Chromium's child services below. Signature-level, so only code
        signed with Cobalt's own key can bind a renderer — which in practice
        means only Cobalt, and that is the point: an exported renderer would let
        any app on the device run code inside Cobalt's sandbox.
    -->
    <permission
        android:name="${applicationId}.permission.CHILD_SERVICE"
        android:protectionLevel="signature" />

    <application
        android:name="${applicationClass}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Cobalt"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="true">
        <!--
            usesCleartextTraffic is declared explicitly rather than left to the
            platform default, because the default changes with targetSdk and a
            browser's cleartext policy must not move underneath it.

            A browser has to be able to load http:// when a user types it: there
            are still hosts that serve nothing else, and refusing them outright
            would be a browser that cannot browse. Cobalt never *upgrades into*
            cleartext on its own - a typed bare host always becomes https (see
            Url.normalize) - so reaching http requires the user to ask for it by
            name. Stage 9 revisits this with a per-site policy and an interstitial.
        -->

        <!--
            The Compose-over-SurfaceView spike (docs/shell-integration.md).
            Not exported, so it is not product surface. That also means
            `adb shell am start` cannot reach it and launching needs root; see
            the class KDoc.

            android:hardwareAccelerated is left at the default deliberately.
            A SurfaceView's compositing behaviour is exactly what is under test,
            and overriding it here would test something else.
        -->
        <!--
            Brings up Chromium's browser process from this app. Not exported,
            so launching needs root; see the class KDoc. Present only in debug
            builds in spirit, but kept here because the manifest has no build
            variant split yet and an unexported activity is not reachable.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".content.ChromiumStartupActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode" />

        <activity
            android:name=".content.ContentSurfaceSpikeActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode" />

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- Cobalt can be opened with a link, but is not registered as a
                 default browser until Stage 7 gives it somewhere to put tabs. -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="http" />
                <data android:scheme="https" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
            The page spike. One tab, one URL, rendered by Chromium. Not
            exported, like the startup spike: it is a development surface, and
            an exported activity that loads whatever URL it is handed is a way
            for other apps to drive the browser.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".content.ChromiumPageActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode|density"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true" />

        <!--
            Chromium's child processes.

            Chromium is multi-process: renderers, the GPU process and the
            utility processes each run in their own OS process, and on Android
            an OS process is a Service. There is no way to start one that is
            not declared here, so the browser process comes up, asks to launch
            a renderer, and dies:

              FATAL EXCEPTION: Chrome_ProcessLauncherThread
              Caused by: PackageManager$NameNotFoundException: ComponentInfo{
                app.auriel.cobalt.nightly/
                org.chromium.content.app.SandboxedProcessService0}

            The classes exist — content ships SandboxedProcessService0..39 and
            PrivilegedProcessService0..9 as real classes, one per slot — but a
            manifest declaration is the app's to make, and Chrome's own is a
            Jinja template (chrome/android/java/AndroidManifest.xml) that the
            Gradle build has no equivalent of. So the entries are written out.

            The counts match Chrome's exactly, 40 and 10, and they are not
            arbitrary on either side: ChildProcessLauncherHelper reads the
            NUM_SANDBOXED_SERVICES and NUM_PRIVILEGED_SERVICES meta-data below
            and will not allocate a slot beyond them, while a slot with no
            class behind it fails the same way as no declaration at all. The
            three numbers — meta-data, declarations, classes in the AAR —
            have to agree.

            sandboxed_process0 carries useAppZygote, as Chrome's does: the app
            zygote pre-forks a renderer so the first one starts faster.
            isolatedProcess is what makes a renderer a renderer — no
            permissions, no shared uid, its own sandbox. Privileged services
            are not isolated, because the GPU process needs the app's own
            access to drivers and surfaces.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="org.chromium.content.browser.NUM_SANDBOXED_SERVICES"
            android:value="40" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="org.chromium.content.browser.NUM_PRIVILEGED_SERVICES"
            android:value="10" />

        <service
            android:name="org.chromium.content.app.SandboxedProcessService0"
            android:process=":sandboxed_process0"
            android:permission="${applicationId}.permission.CHILD_SERVICE"
            android:isolatedProcess="true"
            android:useAppZygote="true"
            android:exported="false" />
